  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The National Aeronautics and Space Administration obligated $15.4B in FY2026 to date. The agency recorded 8,076 contract awards with an average award size of $1.9M, and obligations were down 2.5% year over year. Obligations by fiscal year show a multiyear pattern: FY21 obligations were $15B, FY22 obligations were $15.2B, FY23 obligations were $16.1B, FY24 obligations were $15B, FY25 obligations were $15.8B, and FY26 obligations are $15.4B. Research and development work dominates spending: Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Nanotechnology and Biotechnology) totaled $5.6B in FY2026 to date. Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing totaled $3.2B. The largest contractors include CALIFORNIA INSTITUTE OF TECHNOLOGY with $2.1B and THE BOEING COMPANY with $1B, followed by commercial space and defense primes such as BLUE ORIGIN, LLC with $773M and SPACE EXPLORATION TECHNOLOGIES CORP. with $684M. The distribution of spend highlights heavy investment in R&D and space vehicle manufacturing alongside facilities, engineering, and support services.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How did obligations change year over year?",
        "body": "The National Aeronautics and Space Administration obligated $15.4B in FY26, down 2.5% year over year. Fiscal history shows FY21 obligations were $15B, FY22 obligations were $15.2B, FY23 obligations were $16.1B, FY24 obligations were $15B, and FY25 obligations were $15.8B. The current-year total is a fiscal year to date figure. The award count for FY26 is 8,076 with an average award size of $1.9M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which suppliers and product categories received the most funding?",
        "body": "Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Nanotechnology and Biotechnology) received $5.6B, the largest single NAICS category. Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing received $3.2B, and Engineering Services received $796M. CALIFORNIA INSTITUTE OF TECHNOLOGY received $2.1B and THE BOEING COMPANY received $1B, with BLUE ORIGIN, LLC and SPACE EXPLORATION TECHNOLOGIES CORP. receiving $773M and $684M respectively."
      }
